Under the assumed decomposition with both summands infinite, $$\label{eq:src1}
 \frac{\sqrt Y}{(\log Y)^3}\ll A(Y),B(Y)
 \ll \sqrt Y(\log Y)^2.$$

Let $b\ge1$ be fixed. Suppose $\mu,\nu$ are probability measures on $$A\cap(\sqrt X+N_*,X],\qquad
 D\cap[-X,-\sqrt X-N_*),$$ respectively, and that every point mass is at most $(\log X)^b/\sqrt X$. Put $Q=\sqrt X/(\log X)^{b+1}$. Then $$\label{eq:src2}
\begin{split}
 \sum_{p\le Q}\log p\bigg(
 &\|\mu_p-U_{S_p}\|_2^2+\|\nu_p-U_{S_p^c}\|_2^2\\
 &+\frac{\sigma_p^{-1}+(1-\sigma_p)^{-1}-4}{p}
 \bigg)\ll_b\log\log X.
\end{split}$$ All terms on the left are nonnegative.

Under the assumed eventual decomposition of the primes, $$\label{eq:src3}
 \sum_{T\leq \log p\leq 2T}\frac{\log p}{p}
 \max_{t\in\mathbb F_p}
 \left|\mathbb E_{x\in S_p}\chi_p(x-t)\right|=o(T)
 \qquad (T\longrightarrow\infty).$$

For fixed $0<\alpha<\beta$, as $L_*\longrightarrow\infty$, $$\sum_{\alpha L_*\leq\log\log p\leq\beta L_*}\frac1p
       \max_{t\in\mathbb F_p}
       \left|\mathbb E_{x\in S_p}\chi_p(x-t)\right|=o(L_*).$$

For every fixed $0<\alpha<\beta$, one has $$\label{eq:src16}
 \sum_{\alpha L\le \log\log p\le\beta L}\frac1p
 \max_{\substack{t\in\mathbb F_p,\ \lambda\ {\rm multiplicative}\\
                  \mathop{\mathrm{ord}}(\lambda)>2}}
 \left|\mathbb E_{x\in S_p}\lambda(x-t)\right|=o(L)
 \qquad(L\longrightarrow\infty).$$

Define $$F_p(x)=\frac{\mathbf 1_{S_p}(x)-\sigma_p}
                {\sqrt{\sigma_p(1-\sigma_p)}},
 \qquad
 g_p(v)=\sqrt p\,\mathbb E_{x\in\mathbb F_p}F_p(x)e_p(-vx).$$ For every fixed $0<\alpha<\beta$, outside a set of primes of harmonic mass $o(L)$ in $\alpha L\le\log\log p\le\beta L$, one has uniformly $$\label{eq:src27}
 \sigma_p=\tfrac12+o(1),\qquad
 \max_{\substack{a\in\mathbb F_p\\\lambda\ {\rm multiplicative}}}
 \left|\mathbb E_{v\in\mathbb F_p}g_p(v)\lambda(v)e_p(av)\right|=o(1).$$

There is an absolute constant $\delta_0>0$ such that, for all sufficiently large $L$, $$\label{eq:src28}
 \sum_{\substack{.05L\le \log\log p\le .9L\\
                  1/3\le \sigma_p\le2/3\\
                  \gamma_p\ge\delta_0}}
        \frac1p \ \ge\ .15L .$$

For sufficiently small $\varepsilon_0$, uniformly for $p\in\mathcal S$ and complex $u,v$ with $|u|,|v|\le1.03$, $$\|L_p(u,v)\|\le1+C/p,\qquad |U_p(u,v)|\le1+C/p.$$ For all sufficiently large such primes, $$\label{eq:src31}
 \|L_p(1,1)\|\le U_p(1,1)(1-1.6/p),
 \qquad U_p(1,1)=1+O(\varepsilon_0/p).$$ All constants are absolute.

In a single diagram let the leaf variables be independent uniform elements of $U$. Then $$\label{eq:src37}
                       \mathbb E|W|^2\leq3^r.$$ For two diagrams of the same depth, partition the leaves in each into $b$ nonempty sets, with the sets in the two diagrams paired. Give the two collections of leaf variables the uniform distribution on the subgroup specified by equality of the products on each paired set. The diagrams may have different unit parameters and different conjugation choices. If $l\geq2$ and $b\leq3r/4$, then under <a href="#eq:src35" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:src35">[eq:src35]</a>, uniformly in these choices, $$\label{eq:src38}
                         |\mathbb EW_1\overline{W_2}|=o_l(1).$$ More precisely, the left side is at most $C_l(\varepsilon_q+q^{-1/4})$ for a constant depending only on $l$.

Fix a row of <a href="#eq:arith-progression-scales" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:arith-progression-scales">[eq:arith-progression-scales]</a>. There is at most one primitive real character $\chi_*$, with conductor at most $\exp(\exp(\mu L))$, and one associated real zero $\beta_*<1$ which must be retained in the following formula. If $$\log M_*\leq e^{\mu L},\qquad (a,M_*)=1,$$ and $I$ is an interval of length at most one in $[e^{a_0L},e^{a_1L}]$, then $$\label{eq:src57}
 \sum_{\substack{p\ \text{prime}\,,\ \log p\in I\\p\equiv a\pmod{M_*}}}
       \frac1p
 =\frac1{\phi(M_*)}\int_I
       \bigl(1-\chi_*(a)e^{(\beta_*-1)t}\bigr)\frac{dt}{t}
   +O\bigl(\exp(-c e^{\delta'L})\bigr).$$ The exceptional term is omitted if no such character exists or its conductor does not divide $M_*$. The implied constant is absolute for the fixed row. The multiplier inside the integral lies in $[0,2]$.
